{# ===========================================================================
   Theme init. Must run BEFORE first paint, so it is a blocking, non-module
   inline script and it sits above the stylesheet links in the head.

   /src/js/main.js cannot do this job: type="module" is deferred by spec, so
   it runs after parse and can only correct the attribute after first paint,
   which is a visible flash of the wrong theme.

   TWO RULES, IN THIS ORDER, AND THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE DESIGN.

   1. A STORED CHOICE WINS AND KEEPS WINNING. If this visitor has ever used
      the toggle, that value is applied and the clock is not consulted at all,
      today or ever. A toggle that quietly undoes itself at 18:00 reads as a
      bug, and the fix for that is not a longer expiry, it is never running
      the clock again for someone who has expressed a preference.

   2. WITH NO STORED CHOICE, THE CLOCK DECIDES, in America/Los_Angeles.
      Night from 18:00 to 06:00, day from 06:00 to 18:00.

   WHY THOSE TIMES, AND WHY THAT TIMEZONE. The zone is the restaurant's, not
   the visitor's: the site should be dark when Tajima is in its evening, not
   when someone in Berlin is in theirs. 18:00 is after sunset in San Diego for
   roughly half the year and is squarely inside evening service at every room,
   which run to 22:00 and to 23:30 on Friday and Saturday. 06:00 is before any
   room opens, the earliest being Plaza Bonita at 10:00, so the flip back to
   day never happens while a room is serving.

   FIXED HOURS RATHER THAN ACTUAL SUNSET, deliberately. Sunset in San Diego
   moves between about 16:45 and 20:00 across the year, so a solar calculation
   would track the season more closely. It would also put arithmetic, and a
   failure mode, in a blocking script that runs before the page paints. A
   fixed boundary is deterministic, is two comparisons, and cannot be wrong in
   a way anyone notices.

   Both try/catch blocks matter. localStorage throws in Safari private mode
   and under some storage-partitioning settings; Intl with a named timeZone is
   universal now but this script must never be the reason a page fails to
   paint. Either catch leaves the server-rendered value from site.json in
   place, which is the correct degradation.

   Still deliberately NOT here: prefers-color-scheme. The clock is the
   restaurant's local evening, which is a different question from what the
   visitor's OS is set to, and answering both would mean deciding which wins.

   The writer half lives in /src/js/theme.js. These two share only the key.
   =========================================================================== #}
<script>
  (function () {
    var KEY = "tajima:mode";
    var root = document.documentElement;

    try {
      var stored = localStorage.getItem(KEY);
      if (stored === "day" || stored === "night") {
        root.dataset.mode = stored;
        return;
      }
    } catch (e) {}

    try {
      var hour = Number(
        new Intl.DateTimeFormat("en-US", {
          timeZone: "America/Los_Angeles",
          hour: "numeric",
          hour12: false
        }).format(new Date())
      );
      // Some engines format midnight as 24 rather than 0 under hour12:false.
      if (hour === 24) hour = 0;
      if (hour >= 0 && hour <= 23) {
        root.dataset.mode = hour >= 18 || hour < 6 ? "night" : "day";
      }
    } catch (e) {}
  })();
</script>
